WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.603
Nonessential Elements: Commas, Dashes, Parentheses

00:00:04.983 --> 00:00:07.755
Extra info gets a matching pair of marks.

00:00:08.955 --> 00:00:10.045
How to spot it

00:00:10.445 --> 00:00:13.135
A comma or dash already opens a phrase

00:00:13.685 --> 00:00:15.673
Choices that close it differently

00:00:16.223 --> 00:00:20.257
It’s testing: where extra information starts and ends

00:00:21.457 --> 00:00:22.223
The method

00:00:22.623 --> 00:00:25.268
Step 1: Find the extra info

00:00:25.548 --> 00:00:26.680
Could you delete it?

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.890
Step 2: Delete it in your head

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:32.061
Is the sentence still complete?

00:00:32.611 --> 00:00:35.101
Step 3: Match the opening mark

00:00:35.381 --> 00:00:39.666
Dash opens? Dash closes. Comma? Comma.

00:00:40.216 --> 00:00:43.784
Step 4: Title + name = no commas

00:00:44.064 --> 00:00:46.073
biologist Rachel Carson

00:00:46.623 --> 00:00:47.717
In a few words

00:00:47.867 --> 00:00:50.787
Find it. Delete it. Match it.

00:00:51.987 --> 00:00:52.769
The rules

00:00:53.169 --> 00:00:54.289
A matching pair

00:00:54.569 --> 00:00:58.390
Wrong: The Nile, Africa’s longest river flows north.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:02.595
Right: The Nile, Africa’s longest river, flows north.

00:01:03.145 --> 00:01:04.480
Never mix marks

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.059
Wrong: The lake — the deepest, freezes.

00:01:07.339 --> 00:01:09.691
Right: The lake — the deepest — freezes.

00:01:10.241 --> 00:01:13.096
Title before a name: no commas

00:01:13.376 --> 00:01:15.588
Wrong: astronaut, Sally Ride

00:01:15.868 --> 00:01:18.402
Right: astronaut Sally Ride

00:01:18.952 --> 00:01:21.427
That = essential, which = extra

00:01:21.707 --> 00:01:25.064
Right: The Nile, which flows north, is long.

00:01:26.264 --> 00:01:27.325
Let’s try one

00:01:27.725 --> 00:01:33.812
The Svalbard Global Seed Vault — a facility built deep inside a mountain on a remote Norwegian island

00:01:33.812 --> 00:01:38.647
______ stores backup copies of more than a million seed samples from around the world.

00:01:39.297 --> 00:01:43.209
Here’s the extra information. It opens with a dash.

00:01:43.759 --> 00:01:49.453
Delete it: the seed vault stores backup copies. That’s still a complete sentence.

00:01:50.003 --> 00:01:54.151
It opened with a dash, so it must close with a dash. Choice B.

00:01:55.352 --> 00:01:56.421
Common traps

00:01:56.821 --> 00:01:58.179
Mismatched marks

00:01:58.459 --> 00:02:01.181
Opened with a dash, closed with a comma.

00:02:01.731 --> 00:02:02.987
The missing closer

00:02:03.267 --> 00:02:05.859
Only one comma around the extra info.

00:02:07.059 --> 00:02:11.062
Nonessential Elements: Commas, Dashes, Parentheses

00:02:11.342 --> 00:02:14.262
Find it. Delete it. Match it.

00:02:14.812 --> 00:02:15.392
Spot it

00:02:15.592 --> 00:02:19.196
A comma or dash opens a phrase; choices close it

00:02:19.746 --> 00:02:20.453
Steps

00:02:20.653 --> 00:02:22.450
1. Find the extra info

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:25.078
2. Delete it: still complete?

00:02:25.358 --> 00:02:27.258
3. Close with the same mark

00:02:27.538 --> 00:02:30.183
4. Title + name: no commas

00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:31.457
Remember

00:02:31.657 --> 00:02:36.442
Extra info: matching pair (, , — — ( ))

00:02:36.722 --> 00:02:38.717
Never mix a dash and a comma

00:02:38.997 --> 00:02:41.441
astronaut Sally Ride (no commas)

00:02:41.721 --> 00:02:45.745
that = essential; which = extra (commas)

00:02:46.295 --> 00:02:46.736
Trap

00:02:46.935 --> 00:02:49.658
Opened with a dash, closed with a comma

00:02:50.408 --> 00:02:53.826
Pause the video now, and copy this page into your notes.
